## Why This Is Asked
Tests palindrome enumeration and multi-substring DP; requires handling non-overlapping constraints and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Palindrome precomputation isPal[l][r] in O(n^2).
- DP states dp[i][t] track best total length for first i chars and t palindromes.
- Transition closes a palindrome ending at i via isPal[l][i-1], adding (i-l+1).
- Backpointers enable reconstruction of the endpoints.
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(n^2 + nk).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute isPal
for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
    isPal[i][j] = s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i + 1][j - 1]);
  }
}

// DP and reconstruction
const NEG = -Infinity;
let dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
let prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
    if (t > 0) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (isPal[l - 1][i - 1] && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== NEG) {
          const cand = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (i - l + 1);
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            prev[i][t] = [l - 1, t - 1, i - 1];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP with a constrained path problem and reconstruction, a common real interview topic for large-scale environments like PayPal or Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP state: i, j, t, dir (0 right, 1 down)
- Turn counting via transition from one dir to the other
- Base cases for first row/column
- Path reconstruction pointers and memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) dp[0][j][0][0] = dp[0][j-1][0][0] + grid[0][j];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) dp[i][0][0][1] = dp[i-1][0][0][1] + grid[i][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromLeftSame = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromLeftTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromLeftSame, fromLeftTurn));
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromTopSame = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromTopTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromTopSame, fromTopTurn));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestDir = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
      if (dp[n-1][m-1][t][d] > best) {
        best = dp[n-1][m-1][t][d]; bestT = t; bestDir = d;
      }
    }
  }
  return {best, bestT, bestDir};
}
```

